==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK|US}} {{IPA|/ˈfɪs.tɪ.kʌfs/}}
* {{audio|en-us-fisticuffs.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{head|en|noun}}

# {{plural of|fisticuff}}
# {{plurale tantum|informal}} An [[impromptu]] fight with the fists, usually between only two people.
#* '''1881''', [[w:Robert Louis Stevenson|Robert Louis Stevenson]], ''Virginibus Puerisque, and Other Papers'', "[[s:Virginibus_Puerisque#CHAPTER_I_-_.22VIRGINIBUS_PUERISQUE.22|Virginibus Puerisque]],"
#*: People who share a cell in the Basti[l]le {{...}} if they do not immediately fall to '''fisticuffs''', will find some possible ground of compromise.
#* '''1890''', [[w:Edmondo de Amicis|Edmondo de Amicis]] translated by Caroline Tilton, ''Holland and Its People'', [[s:Holland and its People/Chapter XII|Chapter XII]]
#*: {{...}}, his head all scarred with the sticks and '''fisticuffs''' which he had got in the taverns at Utrecht, {{...}}
# {{plurale tantum|sports|dated}} [[bare|Bare]]-[[knuckle]]d [[boxing]], a form of boxing done without boxing [[glove]]s or similar padding.
#* '''1870''', [[w:Charles Dicken|Charles Dicken]], ''[[w:The Mystery of Edwin Drood|The Mystery of Edwin Drood]]'', [[s:The Mystery of Edwin Drood/Chapter XVII|Chapter XVII]]
#*: In his college days of athletic exercises, Mr. Crisparkle had known professors of the Noble Art of '''fisticuffs''', {{...}}

